899819618,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(3p - 2(p-4) = 7p + 6\\) for \\(p\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Distribute the \\(-2\\) through the parentheses.\n2. Combine like terms.\n3. Isolate the variable \\(p\\).\n4. Solve for \\(p\\).\n\nLet's start with the given equation:\n\\[3p - 2(p-4) = 7p + 6\\]\n\nFirst, distribute the \\(-2\\) through the parentheses:\n\\[3p - 2p + 8 = 7p + 6\\]\n\nNext, combine like terms on the left side of the equation:\n\\[p + 8 = 7p + 6\\]\n\nNow, we need to isolate the variable \\(p\\). To do this, subtract \\(p\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\\[8 = 6p + 6\\]\n\nNext, subtract 6 from both sides to further isolate the term with \\(p\\):\n\\[2 = 6p\\]\n\nFinally, divide both sides by 6 to solve for \\(p\\):\n\\[p = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\\]\n\nSo, the solution is:\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:422"}
